virglrenderer 1.3.0 on ANGLE: declare integer vertex attributes as integers.

Upstream feeds a pure-integer vertex attribute with glVertexAttribIFormat but,
unless use_integer is on, declares it in the generated GLSL as a float and
reads the bits back with floatBitsToUint. Desktop drivers hand the bits over
regardless. ANGLE on Vulkan converts to the declared type, so the values come
out wrong. Chrome passes its glyph texture coordinates that way (R16G16_UINT),
so every glyph sampled outside its atlas: invisible text on an iPhone, black
boxes on a Mac, images missing too. Upstream already turns use_integer on for
ARM's driver; this adds ANGLE. Scripts/build-virgl.sh applies it.

diff -ruN a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -7560,6 +7560,14 @@
        return false;
    if (strcmp(a, "ARM") == 0)
       return true;
+   /* crate: ANGLE, too. On Vulkan it gives a vertex shader input the type it
+    * is declared with, so an integer attribute (glVertexAttribIFormat) read
+    * through a float declaration and floatBitsToUint comes out as garbage:
+    * Chrome's glyph coordinates are such an attribute, and every glyph then
+    * samples outside the atlas. */
+   const char *renderer = (const char *) glGetString(GL_RENDERER);
+   if (renderer && strstr(renderer, "ANGLE"))
+      return true;
    return false;
 }
 
